comments.body:
> @ajtowns I considered your suggestion in https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36521#discussion_r4531930759, but don't like that it's not explicit what the lifetime of the budget counting is. I use a more strongly typed approach here to add mutable state to streams, but still keep the budget object scoped in P2P code. LMK what you think.

Hmm, the lifetime of the budget there is just the wrapper, which seems fairly explicit to me?

So:

```c++
vRecv >> TX_WITH_WITNESS_BLOCK_LIMIT(block_a) >> TX_WITH_WITNESS_BLOCK_LIMT(tx_b);
```

starts the budget at "(" and ends it at ")", so it would be the same as:

```c++
TxWeightBudget budget{MAX_BLOCK_TXS_WEIGHT};
vRecv >> WithState{budget}(TX_WITH_WITNESS_P2P(block_a));
budget = MAX_BLOCK_TXS_WEIGHT;
vRecv >> WithState{budget}(TX_WITH_WITNESS_P2P(tx_b));
```

To get the same as:

```c++
vRecv >> WithState{budget}(TX_WITH_WITNESS_P2P(block_a)) >> WithState{budget}(TX_WITH_WITNESS_P2P(tx_b));
```

you'd need to write:

```c++
const TransactionSerParams TxWitLimit{.allow_witness = true, .reject_empty = false, .max_weight = MAX_BLOCK_TXS_WEIGHT};
ParamsStream{vrecv, TxWitLimit} << block_a << tx_b;
```

at which point the budget is scoped to the params stream.
